# Host-platform build artifacts — must NOT leak into the Linux build context.
# Without this, `COPY . .` in apps/api/Dockerfile copies the host's own
# node_modules (e.g. macOS @esbuild/darwin-arm64) into the image, and
# `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` does not always replace already-present
# native optional-dependency binaries — causing esbuild/tsx to fail at
# runtime with "you installed esbuild for another platform" inside the
# Linux container. See docs/deployment-runbook.md staging validation notes.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# Every workspace package's dist/ is excluded — apps/api/Dockerfile's build
# stage now builds all of them fresh in-container (Production Step 15; see
# docs/deployment-runbook.md's Production Step 15 entry). Earlier steps
# (2B, 8) deliberately did NOT exclude packages/*/dist and apps/*/dist here,
# shipping host-built dist/ via `COPY . .` instead, because in-container tsc
# was believed to unreliably emit zero files. That workaround only ever
# worked because a host-build step (scripts/ci-staging.sh, or just a
# developer's own working tree) always left dist/ on disk before `docker
# build` ran — it broke on Render, whose `runtime: docker` service builds a
# genuinely fresh checkout with no host-build step, so dist/ never existed
# there. Excluding dist/ here again (for every package, not just apps/*)
# means a stale host-built dist/ can never again mask a broken in-container
# build the way it masked this one on every local Docker build before
# Production Step 15.
/dist/
apps/*/dist/
packages/*/dist/
.next/
out/

# `**/*.tsbuildinfo`, NOT the bare `*.tsbuildinfo` this line used to be —
# ROOT CAUSE FOUND in Production Step 15 (see docs/deployment-runbook.md's
# Production Step 15 entry for the full writeup): a bare pattern with no `/`
# in a .dockerignore only excludes matches at the CONTEXT ROOT under this
# Docker/BuildKit version (empirically verified with a minimal repro — see
# the runbook entry) — unlike a .gitignore, where a bare `*.ext` pattern
# matches at any depth. `*.tsbuildinfo` therefore NEVER excluded any nested
# tsbuildinfo file (packages/*/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo, apps/api/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo,
# etc.) — every one of those was silently copied into every Docker build
# this repo ever ran, on every machine. Each package's tsc runs with
# `"incremental": true` (tsconfig.base.json); finding its own stale
# tsbuildinfo already in place, it correctly (by tsc's own incremental-build
# design) treated the package as already-up-to-date and emitted NOTHING —
# every single time, 100% deterministically. THIS is what Production Steps
# 2B and 8 actually observed and (reasonably, given the evidence available
# then) misdiagnosed as "tsc unreliably emits zero output when run against
# COPY'd source" — a full year of workarounds (host-build-and-ship,
# discarded in this step) for a one-line .dockerignore glob bug. A genuinely
# fresh `git clone` never had this problem (gitignored tsbuildinfo files
# don't exist in a clone at all) — which is exactly why this step's own
# clean-clone verification runs never reproduced the failure, and why the
# bug only surfaced against a real, previously-built working tree (and, by
# the same mechanism, would have hit any Docker build platform working from
# a checkout with leftover local build state — not just Render).
**/*.tsbuildinfo
*.tsbuildinfo

.git/
.env
.env.local
.env.production
.env.staging

uploads/
generated/
*.psd

*.log
logs/
coverage/
.nyc_output/
.test-pgdata/
